\description{
Checks that an environment exists, and that its installed packages
satisfy every package match spec. \code{validate_env()} is a query: it never
creates or changes an environment. With the micromamba backend, it
reports an environment that does not exist without starting a process
or installing micromamba.
When no micromamba binary is installed, the micromamba backend reads
the \code{envs} directory of its data directory and the package records in
\verb{conda-meta/} instead. It never installs micromamba.
}
\details{
It uses the same check as \code{create_env(overwrite = FALSE)}. A spec without
a channel must match a package from \code{channels}, or from any channel when
\code{channels} is \code{NULL} (the default). Given
\code{channels = c(channels, additional_channels)} of a \code{create_env()} call
(by default \code{"conda-forge"}), \code{validate_env()} gives the same answer as
that call, with two exceptions. They exist because \code{validate_env()} only
reads:
\itemize{
\item When \code{method} names a backend that does not own the environment,
\code{validate_env()} warns with class \code{condathis_backend_mismatch_warning}
and checks the environment with its owner. \code{create_env()} aborts with
class \code{condathis_backend_mismatch}.
\item When a backend cannot tell whether the environment exists,
\code{validate_env()} still lists its packages, and reports the listing's
error if it fails. In that case, \code{create_env()} aborts with the class
\code{condathis_backend_probe_failed}.
}
}
